# Cookies

A cookie is a small named value the browser stores for your site and sends
back with every later request. They are how a site remembers a theme, a
language, or which session a visitor belongs to.

The `howdy/cookie` module covers both directions. Reading works like
`howdy/query`: a function looks up one cookie, hands your handler its value,
and answers `400` on your behalf when it is missing or malformed. Writing is
a step in the response pipeline.

## Reading and writing

Read a cookie with `use`, and set or delete one by piping the response:

```gleam
import howdy/controller.{type Context}
import howdy/cookie

pub fn preferences_controller() {
  controller.new("preferences")
  |> controller.get("/", show_theme)
  |> controller.post("/dark", use_dark_theme)
  |> controller.delete("/", clear_theme)
}

fn show_theme(ctx: Context) {
  use theme <- cookie.string_or(ctx, "theme", default: "system")
  controller.text(ctx, theme)
}

fn use_dark_theme(ctx: Context) {
  let options =
    cookie.defaults()
    |> cookie.max_age(60 * 60 * 24 * 30)

  controller.text(ctx, "Preference saved")
  |> cookie.set("theme", "dark", options)
}

fn clear_theme(ctx: Context) {
  controller.text(ctx, "Preference cleared")
  |> cookie.delete("theme", cookie.defaults())
}
```

A first visit to `/preferences` answers `system`. After a `POST` to
`/preferences/dark` the browser holds the cookie for thirty days, and the
same page answers `dark`.

## Reading cookies

Cookie values are always strings, and there are three ways to read one. They
differ only in what happens when the cookie is absent:

- `cookie.string` is required. An absent cookie answers `400`.
- `cookie.optional_string` gives you an `Option`: `Some(value)` when the
  cookie was sent, `None` when it was not.
- `cookie.string_or` takes a `default:` and uses it when the cookie is absent.

A bad cookie stops the handler and answers `400` with a JSON body that says
what was wrong. With `use theme <- cookie.string(ctx, "theme")`:

- No `theme` cookie answers `{"error":"missing cookie theme"}`.
- Two `theme` cookies answer `{"error":"cookie theme must occur only once"}`.
- A value that is not valid percent encoding, such as `theme=%zz`, answers
  `{"error":"cookie theme has invalid encoding"}`.

As with query strings, the optional and default forms are only forgiving
about absence. A duplicated or badly encoded cookie answers `400` from all
three. An empty value counts as present: `theme=` gives `""`, not the
default.

Remember that a cookie comes from the client, so treat its value like any
other input. A visitor can edit, delete or invent cookies, and a required
cookie being absent is normal on a first visit. Prefer `string_or` or
`optional_string` unless a missing cookie really is a bad request.

## Setting cookies

`cookie.set` takes the response, a name, a value and the options, and
returns the response with a `set-cookie` header added. Every other header is
kept, including cookies set earlier in the pipeline, so you can set several:

```gleam
controller.text(ctx, "Welcome back")
|> cookie.set("theme", "dark", cookie.defaults())
|> cookie.set("language", "en-GB", cookie.defaults())
```

Values are percent-encoded when written and decoded when read, so spaces,
punctuation, Unicode and literal percent signs all survive the round trip.
Setting `"dark mode 100%"` sends `theme=dark%20mode%20100%25`, and
`cookie.string` hands back the original text. If something other than Howdy
writes a cookie you read, such as JavaScript in the browser, it should use
the same encoding.

Cookies are limited to about 4KB each and travel with every request, so keep
them small: store an identifier, not the data it identifies.

## Cookie options

`cookie.defaults()` is a safe starting point, and the setters adjust it:

```gleam
let options =
  cookie.defaults()
  |> cookie.max_age(60 * 60 * 24 * 30)
  |> cookie.same_site(cookie.Strict)
  |> cookie.path("/preferences")
```

The defaults give a cookie with these attributes:

```
Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax
```

- `max_age` is the lifetime in seconds. Without it the cookie lasts until
  the browser closes. Zero or a negative number expires it at once.
- `secure` controls `Secure`, which keeps the cookie off plain HTTP. It is
  on by default.
- `http_only` controls `HttpOnly`, which hides the cookie from JavaScript.
  It is on by default; turn it off only when a script in the page needs to
  read the value.
- `same_site` takes `cookie.Lax`, `cookie.Strict` or `cookie.None`, and
  decides whether the cookie is sent on requests that start on another
  site. `Lax`, the default, sends it when a visitor follows a link to you
  but not on cross-site form posts or embedded requests.
- `path` limits the cookie to URLs under a path. It defaults to `/`, the
  whole site.
- `domain` shares the cookie with subdomains. Leave it unset to keep the
  cookie on the current host only.

Browsers refuse a `Secure` cookie on a plain `http://` page, with an
exception for `localhost`. If you develop against another host name over
HTTP, turn it off there and nowhere else:

```gleam
cookie.defaults() |> cookie.secure(False)
```

Options are configuration, not user input, so mistakes in them panic rather
than answering `400`. That covers an invalid cookie name, a path that does
not start with `/`, a domain with characters outside letters, digits, dots
and hyphens, and `SameSite=None` without `Secure`, which browsers reject.
Cookie values never panic, because `set` encodes them.

## Deleting cookies

There is no way to tell a browser to delete a cookie outright. Instead
`cookie.delete` overwrites it with an empty value, `Max-Age=0` and an expiry
in the past, and the browser discards it:

```
theme=; Expires=Thu, 01 Jan 1970 00:00:00 GMT; Max-Age=0; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax
```

A browser only replaces a cookie with the same name, path and domain, so
pass `delete` the same path and domain you used when setting it. A cookie
set with `cookie.path("/preferences")` is not removed by a delete that uses
the default path of `/`.

## Sessions and signing

These functions read and write cookies. They do not sign them, encrypt them
or check that a session is real, so never trust a cookie to say who someone
is. A value such as `user_id=42` can be changed to `user_id=1` by anyone.

For authentication, store a long random session identifier in the cookie,
keep the session itself on the server, and let a
[guard](/docs/controllers/guards) look it up and answer
`401` when it is missing or unknown. Keep the defaults for that cookie:
`HttpOnly` stops scripts stealing it, `Secure` keeps it off plain HTTP, and
`SameSite=Lax` stops other sites posting forms with it attached. Add the
[CSRF middleware](/docs/middleware/csrf) as well, which rejects cross-site
writes outright; the two fail in different ways.

## Testing cookies

`howdy/testing` has a helper for each direction. `testing.cookie` adds a
cookie to a request, encoding the value the same way `cookie.set` does:

```gleam
import howdy/testing

pub fn theme_is_read_test() {
  let res =
    testing.get("/preferences")
    |> testing.cookie("theme", "dark")
    |> testing.send(app())
  assert testing.text(res) == "dark"
}
```

`testing.cookies` lists every cookie a response sets, as decoded name and
value pairs in the order they were set. A deleted cookie appears with an
empty value:

```gleam
import gleam/http

pub fn theme_is_saved_test() {
  let res =
    testing.request(http.Post, "/preferences/dark")
    |> testing.send(app())
  assert testing.cookies(res) == [#("theme", "dark")]
}
```

Attributes such as `Path` and `Max-Age` are left out of that list. To check
them, read the raw `set-cookie` headers from `res.headers`.
